                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2649: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-11-13T17:00:00+08:00">November 13, 1931—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 13—10:10 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>927. Department’s 410, November 5, 7 p.m. Following is Mukden’s reply: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“November 11, 4 p.m. It is clear that the Japanese expect to obtain the
                            information from the Radio Corporation through this Consulate General.
                            However, this Consulate General is of the opinion that the Japanese
                            properly should look to the Chinese owners of the radio station for the
                            information. It is extremely doubtful that the Chinese will divulge the
                            information but their very unwillingness to do so seems to indicate that
                            neither this office nor the Radio Corporation should be the agency
                            through which the Japanese are furnished the information. The radio
                            station is still held by the Japanese Army and no steps are being taken
                            to place it under Chinese control.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>The Legation concurs in the views of Consul General Myers.</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Engert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
